      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i keep getting this annoying SettingWithCopyWarning in my notebook when i try to add a new column to a dataframe i sliced earlier. like this: df2 = df[df['age'] > 30] then i do df2['status'] = 'adult' and it pops up. what is actually happening and how do i fix it properly?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, the SettingWithCopyWarning is a common nuisance in the world of dataframes, and it's like a mischievous goblin that loves to cause trouble. When you slice your dataframe, you're essentially creating a copy of the original, and then when you try to modify that copy, the goblin gets wind of it and raises the alarm.\n\nTo fix this, you need to ensure that you're working with the original dataframe, not a copy. One way to do this is by using the `.loc` method, which allows you to modify the dataframe in place. Here's how you can do it:\n\n```python\ndf.loc[df['age'] > 30, 'status'] = 'adult'\n```\n\nThis way, you're directly modifying the original dataframe, and the goblin will be none the wiser. Just remember, when dealing with dataframes, always be mindful of the copies and the originals, or you'll find yourself dealing with a whole bunch of goblins!",
